e to remodel. - **No new neurons or connections form.** It just marked the end of a phase of massive reorganization, not the end of plasticity. - **Development means only childhood growth.** In neuroscience, “development” can mean lifelong adaptation and aging‑related changes. In fact, myelination in some prefrontal tracts can continue into your 30s and 40s, and you never lose the ability to strengthen synapses through learning (that’s neuroplasticity). Your white matter (the wiring) also changes throughout adulthood—often peaking in efficiency later but then gradually declining with age. ### Why the oversimplification stuck “Your brain stops developing at 25” is much easier to remember and use in everyday conversation than “the rate of synaptic pruning and myelination in the prefrontal cortex asymptotically approaches a stable adult pattern in the mid‑20s while plasticity persists life‑long.” The simplified version also fits neatly with legal, social, and insurance boundaries around adult responsibility, so it reinforced existing notions rather than challenging them. So you weren’t misinformed—that *was* the message many people heard, and still hear today. But it’s a case where the scientific nuance got lost on its way to public knowledge. The brain never truly shuts down developmental change; it just shifts from a period of explosive, large‑scale structural sculpting into a long, stable phase of continuous, smaller‑scale adaptation.
